--TEST--
Every sink a `tainted` or `secret` value can reach refuses it, counted in one program so a sink that stopped checking loses an error — `rule:security/sink-predicate`, `rule:security/every-grammar-is-a-sink`, `rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`, `rule:core-classes/secret-reveal`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:types/qualifiers-tainted-and-secret
// One marked value is offered to every sink in turn, so the assertion is the
// number of refusals rather than any one of them. A sink that quietly started
// accepting a marked value drops an error here while every other line still
// looks right.
//
// The four grammars are the roster `rule:security/every-grammar-is-a-sink`
// fixes: a regex pattern, a format template, a byte-packing format and a date
// pattern. The last `secret` group is every place a value is written down for
// a person to read, and `Core\Str::length` stands for the parameters nobody
// classified (`rule:security/unclassified-parameter-refuses-tainted`).
tainted string $raw = "a+b";
secret string $token = "hunter2";
secret tainted string $both = $token . $raw;

string $plain = $raw;

Core\Regex\Pattern $rx = Core\Regex::compile($raw);
string $formatted = Core\Str::format($raw, 1);
bytes $packed = Core\Bytes::pack($raw, 1);
Core\Time\Date $day = Core\Time\Date::at(2026, 9, 19);
string $stamped = $day->format($raw);

// `reveal` removes `secret` and leaves `tainted` where it was, so this answer
// is still refused by a plain binding (`rule:core-classes/secret-reveal`).
string $revealed = Core\Secret::reveal($both, "pinning what reveal leaves behind");

echo $token, "\n";
print($token);
Core\Debug::dump($token);
string $json = Core\Json::encode($token);
uint $length = Core\Str::length($token);
throw new RuntimeError($token);
--EXPECT--

